1
00:00:00,590 --> 00:00:02,700
In this video,
we're going to talk

2
00:00:02,700 --> 00:00:04,530
about signals in Linux.

3
00:00:04,530 --> 00:00:06,360
We're going to
introduce signals,

4
00:00:06,360 --> 00:00:08,550
understand how defaults
signal handling

5
00:00:08,550 --> 00:00:10,710
works for user
space applications,

6
00:00:10,710 --> 00:00:13,540
and we're going to
introduce core files.

7
00:00:13,610 --> 00:00:15,960
Signals are software interrupts

8
00:00:15,960 --> 00:00:18,015
for handling
asynchronous events.

9
00:00:18,015 --> 00:00:20,055
These can be events
that are coming from

10
00:00:20,055 --> 00:00:22,770
outside the current
program, from the system,

11
00:00:22,770 --> 00:00:24,840
Control C, it could

12
00:00:24,840 --> 00:00:27,300
be events from the
program or kernel itself,

13
00:00:27,300 --> 00:00:29,980
for instance, divide by 0.

14
00:00:30,020 --> 00:00:32,640
Signals can also be used as

15
00:00:32,640 --> 00:00:35,210
a rudimentary form of
inter-process communication,

16
00:00:35,210 --> 00:00:36,420
how to communicate between

17
00:00:36,420 --> 00:00:38,755
two different running processes.

18
00:00:38,755 --> 00:00:41,225
An important thing to know
about signals is that

19
00:00:41,225 --> 00:00:44,120
the event that causes the
signal is asynchronous,

20
00:00:44,120 --> 00:00:46,340
and the handler is
also asynchronous

21
00:00:46,340 --> 00:00:48,590
with respect to
program execution.

22
00:00:48,590 --> 00:00:50,240
That has some
important limitations

23
00:00:50,240 --> 00:00:52,615
on how we handle signals.

24
00:00:52,615 --> 00:00:55,420
The signal life-cycle.

25
00:00:55,730 --> 00:00:58,520
A signal's first raised sent or

26
00:00:58,520 --> 00:00:59,810
generated based on those events

27
00:00:59,810 --> 00:01:01,310
that we talked about previously,

28
00:01:01,310 --> 00:01:02,840
it's stored by the kernel,

29
00:01:02,840 --> 00:01:04,490
and then it's handled
by the kernel

30
00:01:04,490 --> 00:01:06,590
based on the requests
from the process.

31
00:01:06,590 --> 00:01:11,975
The process can request how
to handle specific signals,

32
00:01:11,975 --> 00:01:13,940
and that can be changed based

33
00:01:13,940 --> 00:01:16,720
on the preferences
of the application.

34
00:01:16,720 --> 00:01:19,460
Some of the signal handling
options that can be

35
00:01:19,460 --> 00:01:21,785
set up by the process,

36
00:01:21,785 --> 00:01:23,990
you can ignore for most signals.

37
00:01:23,990 --> 00:01:26,615
There's exceptions for
SIGKILL and SIGSTOP,

38
00:01:26,615 --> 00:01:28,435
which cannot be ignored,

39
00:01:28,435 --> 00:01:32,745
or you can catch and
handle the signal.

40
00:01:32,745 --> 00:01:35,340
When you catch and
handle a signal,

41
00:01:35,340 --> 00:01:37,250
the kernel's going to suspend

42
00:01:37,250 --> 00:01:39,005
execution of the
current process,

43
00:01:39,005 --> 00:01:42,395
and that includes execution
of other signal handlers.

44
00:01:42,395 --> 00:01:44,210
It's possible to get a signal

45
00:01:44,210 --> 00:01:46,720
when you're inside
a signal handler.

46
00:01:46,720 --> 00:01:48,360
Then you're going to jump to

47
00:01:48,360 --> 00:01:50,525
any previously
registered function

48
00:01:50,525 --> 00:01:54,440
that was set up to
handle the signal,

49
00:01:54,440 --> 00:01:56,780
and you're going to

50
00:01:56,780 --> 00:01:59,200
execute the code in
that signal handler.

51
00:01:59,200 --> 00:02:02,060
Basically swapping out
the current thread

52
00:02:02,060 --> 00:02:04,070
or interrupting the
current thread and

53
00:02:04,070 --> 00:02:07,115
replacing the next instruction

54
00:02:07,115 --> 00:02:08,240
with the instruction
that's coming

55
00:02:08,240 --> 00:02:10,735
from the signal handler.

56
00:02:10,735 --> 00:02:13,890
Two example of signal handling

57
00:02:13,890 --> 00:02:18,310
that are really common
are SIGINT and SIGTERM.

58
00:02:18,670 --> 00:02:21,770
The default action is

59
00:02:21,770 --> 00:02:24,980
the third option of how
to handle a signal.

60
00:02:24,980 --> 00:02:27,365
If you don't set up a
specific signal action,

61
00:02:27,365 --> 00:02:28,950
you'll get the default action.

62
00:02:28,950 --> 00:02:30,770
In a lot of cases,
the default action

63
00:02:30,770 --> 00:02:32,495
is just to terminate
the process.

64
00:02:32,495 --> 00:02:33,755
In some cases,

65
00:02:33,755 --> 00:02:35,530
it'll terminate the process,

66
00:02:35,530 --> 00:02:37,395
possibly with a core dump.

67
00:02:37,395 --> 00:02:39,650
A core dump is just a capture

68
00:02:39,650 --> 00:02:40,880
of the running process memory,

69
00:02:40,880 --> 00:02:42,935
which we can use
with tools like GDB

70
00:02:42,935 --> 00:02:46,225
to help figure out what
happened in the program.

71
00:02:46,225 --> 00:02:49,340
Here's an example of
signal default actions

72
00:02:49,340 --> 00:02:50,990
based on the type of signal,

73
00:02:50,990 --> 00:02:53,150
and you can get this from
the signal man-page.

74
00:02:53,150 --> 00:02:55,075
You can see here the signal,

75
00:02:55,075 --> 00:02:58,730
and you can see in this column
the default action where

76
00:02:58,730 --> 00:03:00,470
Term means that it's going to

77
00:03:00,470 --> 00:03:02,875
be sending SIGTERM
to the process,

78
00:03:02,875 --> 00:03:05,115
Ign means it's going
to be ignored,

79
00:03:05,115 --> 00:03:09,000
and Core is going to
mean both that it sends

80
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:11,025
SIGTERM and it also dumps a core

81
00:03:11,025 --> 00:03:13,830
if core file is
configured properly.

82
00:03:13,830 --> 00:03:17,385
Stop means that it
will send SIGSTOP.

83
00:03:17,385 --> 00:03:19,430
This is referencing some of

84
00:03:19,430 --> 00:03:20,930
the signals that we can send,

85
00:03:20,930 --> 00:03:22,490
and there are a whole
list of signals

86
00:03:22,490 --> 00:03:24,800
in Linux system
programming Chapter 10,

87
00:03:24,800 --> 00:03:26,510
but I want to go through some

88
00:03:26,510 --> 00:03:28,465
of the more common ones here.

89
00:03:28,465 --> 00:03:33,805
SIGABRT is what happens when
you use the assert function.

90
00:03:33,805 --> 00:03:36,530
When you put an assertion
inside your code,

91
00:03:36,530 --> 00:03:40,895
if your code can't continue
due to some invalid input and

92
00:03:40,895 --> 00:03:45,350
you can't handle that
particular error

93
00:03:45,350 --> 00:03:47,560
and you need to
terminate the process,

94
00:03:47,560 --> 00:03:50,220
the assert function will

95
00:03:50,220 --> 00:03:53,160
result in a SIGABRT if
the assertion fails.

96
00:03:53,160 --> 00:03:57,575
SIGHUP is one that
stands for hang-up,

97
00:03:57,575 --> 00:04:00,760
is a carry-over from
the old modem days,

98
00:04:00,760 --> 00:04:03,320
but it's often used
as a signal that

99
00:04:03,320 --> 00:04:06,980
tells an application to reread
its configuration files.

100
00:04:06,980 --> 00:04:08,510
If you change something

101
00:04:08,510 --> 00:04:09,770
in the configuration
and you want it

102
00:04:09,770 --> 00:04:11,030
to apply to a process that's

103
00:04:11,030 --> 00:04:12,535
already running like a daemon,

104
00:04:12,535 --> 00:04:14,180
a lot of times the

105
00:04:14,180 --> 00:04:16,520
handling for that will
be through SIGHUB.

106
00:04:16,520 --> 00:04:19,520
SIGINT is Control
C. If you've ever

107
00:04:19,520 --> 00:04:20,960
stopped a program
that's running in

108
00:04:20,960 --> 00:04:22,700
the terminal by using Control C,

109
00:04:22,700 --> 00:04:24,965
what you're really doing
is sending SIGINT.

110
00:04:24,965 --> 00:04:27,785
SIGKILL is something
that you can send,

111
00:04:27,785 --> 00:04:28,850
that can't be ignored,

112
00:04:28,850 --> 00:04:30,610
and unconditionally
terminates the process.

113
00:04:30,610 --> 00:04:33,050
This is useful if you've
got an application

114
00:04:33,050 --> 00:04:36,690
that's not responding that
you need to terminate.

115
00:04:36,950 --> 00:04:40,155
Then SIGSEV is
segmentation fault.

116
00:04:40,155 --> 00:04:42,430
This happens on any null pointer

117
00:04:42,430 --> 00:04:44,825
or invalid memory access,

118
00:04:44,825 --> 00:04:46,540
and the default action
for this one is

119
00:04:46,540 --> 00:04:48,700
terminating and generating
a core file which

120
00:04:48,700 --> 00:04:50,530
can be useful for
helping to find

121
00:04:50,530 --> 00:04:53,065
where the segmentation
fault's occurring.

122
00:04:53,065 --> 00:04:56,190
SIGTERM will gracefully
terminate a process.

123
00:04:56,190 --> 00:04:59,385
This is the nice way of
shutting down a daemon.

124
00:04:59,385 --> 00:05:01,570
You can send SIGTERM
and the daemon

125
00:05:01,570 --> 00:05:03,820
will have the opportunity
to catch that

126
00:05:03,820 --> 00:05:05,500
and do whatever proper

127
00:05:05,500 --> 00:05:09,590
close down that it needs to
do to shut down gracefully.

128
00:05:09,590 --> 00:05:12,565
SIGSTOP is similar to SIGKILL,

129
00:05:12,565 --> 00:05:15,910
except it's pausing the
process instead of killing it,

130
00:05:15,910 --> 00:05:17,320
and it's similar in the state

131
00:05:17,320 --> 00:05:19,940
that it sends that
they can't be ignored.

132
00:05:20,810 --> 00:05:24,040
A little bit more when we talk

133
00:05:24,040 --> 00:05:26,935
about core dump
files and SIGSEV,

134
00:05:26,935 --> 00:05:29,665
how we can use the capabilities

135
00:05:29,665 --> 00:05:32,795
of the core file to help figure
out where the problem is.

136
00:05:32,795 --> 00:05:35,380
Here's an example of a
really simple application

137
00:05:35,380 --> 00:05:37,165
that has a segmentation fault.

138
00:05:37,165 --> 00:05:40,120
In order to create an
invalid memory access,

139
00:05:40,120 --> 00:05:41,770
we can just initialize
a pointer to

140
00:05:41,770 --> 00:05:44,575
zero and then try to
do anything with it.

141
00:05:44,575 --> 00:05:46,970
In this case, we're
trying to add one to it.

142
00:05:46,970 --> 00:05:48,375
We're trying to add

143
00:05:48,375 --> 00:05:50,980
1 to 0 location in

144
00:05:50,980 --> 00:05:54,040
memory which is not going to
be allocated to our process.

145
00:05:54,040 --> 00:05:56,980
That's going to result
in a segmentation fault.

146
00:05:56,980 --> 00:06:01,235
Attempts to access. Obviously,

147
00:06:01,235 --> 00:06:04,145
with this really
simple application,

148
00:06:04,145 --> 00:06:07,175
it wouldn't be very
hard to find the issue,

149
00:06:07,175 --> 00:06:11,675
but in more complicated
applications,

150
00:06:11,675 --> 00:06:15,110
the built-in capabilities
of the core file in GDB can

151
00:06:15,110 --> 00:06:18,710
be used to take you
directly to the issue,

152
00:06:18,710 --> 00:06:21,605
which is very
helpful debug tool.

153
00:06:21,605 --> 00:06:23,600
There's a certain amount
of setup that you need to

154
00:06:23,600 --> 00:06:26,705
do in order to use
the core file,

155
00:06:26,705 --> 00:06:29,075
that's the default for SIGSEV.

156
00:06:29,075 --> 00:06:30,740
Even though the
default for the signal

157
00:06:30,740 --> 00:06:32,090
is to create a core file,

158
00:06:32,090 --> 00:06:33,635
depending on your distribution,

159
00:06:33,635 --> 00:06:35,270
you might have
some work to do to

160
00:06:35,270 --> 00:06:37,865
set up to actually
generate the core file.

161
00:06:37,865 --> 00:06:39,350
These are the steps you can use

162
00:06:39,350 --> 00:06:43,350
on Ubuntu to set up core files.

163
00:06:43,350 --> 00:06:44,750
There's a link here
at the bottom that

164
00:06:44,750 --> 00:06:46,370
takes you through each
one of these steps.

165
00:06:46,370 --> 00:06:48,620
But basically, we're setting
up the kernel to tell

166
00:06:48,620 --> 00:06:51,100
it how and where to
generate the core file,

167
00:06:51,100 --> 00:06:54,390
and then we're running
the application,

168
00:06:54,390 --> 00:06:56,535
and you'll see here
that there was

169
00:06:56,535 --> 00:06:59,335
a segmentation fault
core dump message.

170
00:06:59,335 --> 00:07:02,750
If we look at the apport.log,

171
00:07:02,750 --> 00:07:05,780
which is what Ubuntu uses
for generating crash logs,

172
00:07:05,780 --> 00:07:08,420
you can see that we've
created a log with

173
00:07:08,420 --> 00:07:11,450
a specific reference to

174
00:07:11,450 --> 00:07:13,100
the core that we just

175
00:07:13,100 --> 00:07:15,320
created with our
segmentation fault.

176
00:07:15,320 --> 00:07:18,590
If we use apport-unpack that,

177
00:07:18,590 --> 00:07:20,825
we can unpack that
back into a core file,

178
00:07:20,825 --> 00:07:24,575
and then we can use GDB
to look at the core dump.

179
00:07:24,575 --> 00:07:28,885
If you look at this
core dump and GDB,

180
00:07:28,885 --> 00:07:30,980
you can see that it's
going to point us right to

181
00:07:30,980 --> 00:07:32,840
the place where the
segmentation fault occurs.

182
00:07:32,840 --> 00:07:34,970
It's going to show us
that it was this line,

183
00:07:34,970 --> 00:07:36,485
mem, equals men plus 1.

184
00:07:36,485 --> 00:07:38,440
This can be a
really useful tool.

185
00:07:38,440 --> 00:07:40,010
Even though segmentation faults

186
00:07:40,010 --> 00:07:41,270
might look a little scary when

187
00:07:41,270 --> 00:07:44,120
you see this message
that saying core dump,

188
00:07:44,120 --> 00:07:46,880
you can use GDB
and core tools to

189
00:07:46,880 --> 00:07:48,170
really help you
find these pretty

190
00:07:48,170 --> 00:07:50,790
easily in a lot of cases.